WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
Secrets change. Sometimes they change on a schedule.

2
00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:11.557
Sometimes they change because a person leaves, a token expires, a provider rotates a key, or an incident requires revocation.

3
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:18.445
A Go service that assumes credentials never change will eventually turn a routine rotation into an outage.

4
00:00:18.445 --> 00:00:23.496
Rotation needs to be part of the design, not a surprising production exercise.

5
00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:32.603
Static secrets, such as a long-lived database password or webhook signing secret, are simple to understand but risky when they live too long.

6
00:00:32.603 --> 00:00:36.966
They need an owner, rotation interval, and replacement procedure.

7
00:00:36.966 --> 00:00:45.155
Dynamic or short-lived credentials reduce exposure time because they expire automatically, but they require refresh logic.

8
00:00:45.155 --> 00:00:56.788
A service using dynamic database credentials, for example, must understand when connections need to be recreated and how to handle credentials that expire while work is in flight.

9
00:00:56.788 --> 00:01:03.446
Caching is a tradeoff. A local cache reduces latency and avoids calling the provider for every request.

10
00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:12.707
It can also reduce provider load and make the service more resilient during brief provider interruptions. But cached secrets can become stale.

11
00:01:12.707 --> 00:01:20.054
They can delay revocation. They can cause multiple service instances to use different versions during a rollout.

12
00:01:20.054 --> 00:01:28.473
The cache needs a time-to-live, refresh behavior, and error policy that matches the risk of the secret and the needs of the service.

13
00:01:28.473 --> 00:01:31.152
Refresh logic should avoid two extremes.

14
00:01:31.152 --> 00:01:38.269
One extreme is panic on any provider error, which can take down healthy traffic during a short provider outage.

15
00:01:38.269 --> 00:01:43.856
The other extreme is use an old secret forever, which defeats rotation and revocation.

16
00:01:43.856 --> 00:01:57.096
A better design is explicit: use a valid cached value until its allowed lifetime ends, refresh in the background when possible, fail closed when no valid value exists, and report metrics when refresh fails.

17
00:01:57.096 --> 00:02:02.760
The exact policy depends on the system, but the policy should be written down and tested.

18
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:21.434
Common failure modes include an expired provider token, a revoked credential, a provider outage, a partial rollout where only some instances receive the new value, a rotated database password while old connections remain open, and clients that verify signatures with only one active key.

19
00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:27.327
Many production systems need overlap windows, where old and new secrets both work briefly.

20
00:02:27.327 --> 00:02:31.690
Others need immediate revocation, where overlap is unacceptable.

21
00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:45.466
For Go engineers, the rotation question is practical: how does this dependency learn about a new secret, how does it replace old state, how does it behave when refresh fails, and how will operators see what happened?

22
00:02:45.466 --> 00:02:53.655
If those answers are clear before an incident, rotation becomes maintenance instead of drama. Rotation should be observable.

23
00:02:53.655 --> 00:03:04.752
Operators need to know when the last refresh succeeded, which version or generation is active when that information is safe to expose, and whether failures are increasing.

24
00:03:04.752 --> 00:03:09.803
Metrics and logs should describe refresh health without printing the secret.

25
00:03:09.803 --> 00:03:18.528
This lets teams detect stale credentials, provider outages, or partial rollout problems before customers experience a hard failure.
